Fix Fortran REAL*16 / COMPLEX*32 (MPI_REAL16 / MPI_COMPLEX32) in OpenMPI 5.0.11.

5.0.11 changed the C type backing Fortran REAL*16 and COMPLEX*32 from
`long double` / `long double _Complex` to `_Float128` / `_Float128 _Complex`.
The REAL*16 half is a real bug fix: on x86_64 gfortran's REAL*16 is IEEE
binary128 while C's `long double` is the x87 80-bit format, so MPI_REAL16
reductions were being done in the wrong format.  But COMPLEX(K) is by
definition a pair of REAL(K), so the two mappings have to agree -- and the
COMPLEX*32 half of the change was left incomplete at three levels:

1. configure: OMPI_FORTRAN_CHECK's C-type -> ISO_C_BINDING KIND case
   statement gained entries for `_Float128`/`__float128` but not for their
   _Complex forms, so `ofc_type_kind` keeps its default value of 0, the
   `-le 0` check fires, and configure prints

       configure: WARNING: Compiler COMPLEX*32 and _Float128 _Complex
       mismatch; MPI datatype unsupported

   leaving OMPI_HAVE_FORTRAN_COMPLEX32=0 -- MPI_COMPLEX32 silently becomes
   MPI_DATATYPE_NULL.  (Verified against a native 5.0.11 build with
   gcc/gfortran 11.4 on x86_64; 5.0.10 gives OMPI_HAVE_FORTRAN_COMPLEX32=1.)

2. ompi/datatype: OMPI_KIND_FORTRAN_COMPLEX32 is token-pasted into
   OMPI_DATATYPE_INITIALIZER_##TYPE, and no C_FLOAT128_COMPLEX alias exists,
   so ompi_datatype_module.c fails to compile.  The underlying OPAL type is
   already fully implemented (OPAL_DATATYPE_FLOAT128_COMPLEX = 27, with its
   initializer, copy functions, pack/unpack cases, size table and datatype
   object) and ompi_mpi_complex32 is already registered at
   OMPI_DATATYPE_MPI_COMPLEX32 -- only the OMPI -> OPAL alias is missing.

3. ompi/op: ompi_op_ddt_map[] maps MPI_COMPLEX32 to
   OMPI_OP_BASE_TYPE_##OMPI_KIND_FORTRAN_COMPLEX32, and there is no
   OMPI_OP_BASE_TYPE_C_FLOAT128_COMPLEX, so ompi/op/op.c fails to compile.
   Add the enum value, the corresponding MPI_SUM / MPI_PROD reduction
   functions (2-buffer and 3-buffer), and the dispatch-table entry, all
   following the existing c_long_double_complex pattern and guarded the same
   way OPAL guards its float128 complex support.

With this, MPI_COMPLEX32 is exactly a pair of MPI_REAL16, the Fortran and C
types agree, and reductions are performed in binary128 rather than x87.

Platforms whose Fortran REAL*16 is not IEEE binary128 -- notably
powerpc64le, where it is IBM extended double ("double-double") matching C
`long double` -- must pin opal_cv_find_type_REAL_16 and
opal_cv_find_type_COMPLEX_32 to the `long double` forms instead; see
bundled/files/powerpc64le-linux-gnu.

NOTE: this patch deliberately touches only the generated `configure`, and not
config/ompi_fortran_check.m4 / config/ompi_setup_mpi_fortran.m4 which it was
generated from.  Patching the m4 sources makes them newer than aclocal.m4 /
configure / Makefile.in, which triggers automake's maintainer-mode
regeneration during `make`; the build environment has no usable
aclocal/autoconf/automake, so that fails with
"possibly undefined macro: AC_MSG_ERROR".  The other three files are plain
C sources/headers and are not autotools inputs, so patching them is safe.

